</section>
<section id="A8" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix H </span>Estimator Variance Analysis</h2>

<figure id="A8.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.22500v1/Figure/agent_details.png" id="A8.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/209;" width="548" height="209" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="A8.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Estimator sensitivity under weak signals.</span>
Detailed comparison of raw complementary accuracy (<math id="A8.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>) and its linear transform (trans) across GPQA, Math-MC, and Medical Abstract.
The figure illustrates how the two estimators behave differently depending on task difficulty, with variance amplification explaining when the transform helps or hurts.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A8.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Appendix H Estimator Variance Analysis ‣ Scalable Oversightvia Partitioned Human Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> reports ADAS and AFlow under the two estimators—raw complementary accuracy (<math id="A8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>) and its linear transform (trans)—each selected at the iteration that maximizes validation accuracy.
The results show clear regime dependence: on <span id="A8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPQA</span> (the hardest benchmark), <math id="A8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is more reliable (e.g., ADAS drops from 47.2 with <math id="A8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> to 42.4 with trans, while AFlow remains unchanged at 47.2).
On <span id="A8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Math-MC</span>, ADAS still prefers <math id="A8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> (88.1 vs. 84.2), whereas AFlow shows only a slight gain with trans (86.8 vs. 86.5).
On <span id="A8.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Medical Abstract</span>, where complementary accuracy is relatively high, the transform becomes beneficial: ADAS improves from 61.6 to 67.8, and AFlow from 68.5 to 68.9.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This behavior is explained by variance amplification: according to Eq. (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.1 Estimator from Complementary Labels ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ Scalable Oversightvia Partitioned Human Supervision" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), the variance of the transformed estimator scales by <math id="A8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K\!-\!1)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K\!-\!1)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> compared to <math id="A8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.
When signals are weak (e.g., GPQA), variance amplification exacerbates noise and harms performance.
In settings with stronger signals (Math-MC, Medical Abstract), the effect is less detrimental, and the transformed estimator occasionally yields modest gains.
A systematic study of this trade-off is left for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
